# mm.lcd.st7789

The Sitronix ST7789 colour TFT controller, over portable mm.mcu SPI and GPIO. It
contains no vendor header and no board pin number: a board's provider supplies
the wiring, the panel timings, and the initialisation sequence, and this module
drives the protocol.

Pixels are RGB565, most significant byte first, which is what the controller
accepts once its pixel format is set to sixteen bits. geometry reports sixteen
bits per pixel and mm.display's packed-write contract does the rest: write takes
the bytes the caller composed and puts them in the window it names.

clear takes mm.display's three named colours and maps them onto their RGB565
values. Those three are what a portable smoke test can ask of any panel; a
caller that wants one of the other sixty-five thousand composes pixels and
writes them.

refresh answers Ok and sends nothing. This panel shows what was written when it
was written, so a caller asking for the frame to be visible is asking for
something already true. Answering Unsupported would be wrong, and would make one
portable sequence impossible to write across an ePaper panel and a TFT.

Modelled on Waveshare's LCD_2in8 driver for the RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8. Its scan
direction and its full-framebuffer Display path are absent: mm.display writes
rectangles, and rotation belongs to the caller for the same reason it does in
mm.touch.
